#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bessel_y_zero

#% text
# `bessel_y_zero(nu, k)` gives the `k`-th positive zero of the Bessel function `Y_nu`; `bessel_y_zero(nu, k, x0)` gives the `k`-th zero above `x0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For a non-integer `k = m - t` the value is a zero of `Y_nu cos(pi t) + J_nu sin(pi t)`: the `m`-th positive one for `t < 1/2`, the `(m - 1)`-th for `t >= 1/2` (the first zero of the family enters the origin at `t = 1/2`), continuous in `k`.

#% item
# Numerical values: the sign changes are counted in machine arithmetic, the bracket bisected, and the zero refined by Newton's method at the working precision; exact arguments stay unevaluated and `n` gives the values.

#% item
# `nu > -1`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
n(bessel_y_zero(0, 1))
#% output
#= 0.8935769662791675

#% cell
n(bessel_y_zero(0, 2))
#% output
#= 3.957678419314858

#% cell
n(bessel_y_zero(1, 10, 50), 30)
#% output
#= 80.89137529327611329860122080154373

#% cell
n(bessel_y_zero(0, 2/3), 20)
#% output
#= 0.073819598152447496768098

#% cell
bessel_y(0, n(bessel_y_zero(0, 3), 30))
#% output
#= ball(9.3964057169798394931e-36, 1.40e-32)

